SHA-256 is a one-way hash function \textcolor{yellow}{(more precisely: preimage-resistant)}, and therefore Eve's knowledge of the hash value of the first 1000 binary digits of d does not reveal any practically useful information about d. \textcolor{green}{In particular, she cannot feasibly recover those bits: a preimage attack on SHA-256 costs about 2^256 operations, and knowing only the digest does not let her test which 1000-bit preimage is the true prefix of d.} \textcolor{green}{Information-theoretically, the 256-bit digest leaks at most 256 bits of mutual information about the 1000-bit prefix, but it does not determine any specific bit positions.}

Eve does have some information about d that is given by d obeying d ≡ e^{-1} mod φ(N) \textcolor{green}{(equivalently, ed ≡ 1 mod λ(N), where λ(N) = lcm(p−1, q−1); using φ(N) here is standard and fine)}, but this is not enough to learn any bits of d without knowing φ(N); and knowing φ(N) would allow her to probabilistically factor N. \textcolor{red}{Knowing φ(N) lets you deterministically factor N in polynomial time via p+q = N − φ(N) + 1 and solving x^2 − (p+q)x + N = 0.}

Therefore, unless N is of some special form, Eve cannot learn any bit-values of d from this information. \textcolor{green}{Also, known partial key exposure attacks (e.g., with small e) require actual contiguous bits of d, not just a hash of them.} \textcolor{red}{Missing explicit quantitative answer: the number of bit-values of d she can learn is 0 (with overwhelming probability).}

Wir haben $\varphi(N) = (p-1)(q-1) = N - p - q + 1$, also $\varphi(N) \approx N$, mit einem Fehler in der Größenordnung $\sqrt{N}$ (da $p$ und $q$ etwa gleichgroß sind). \textcolor{green}{Genauer: $\delta:=N-\varphi(N)=p+q-1<2\sqrt{N}$; bei 1024‑Bit‑Primzahlen hat $\delta$ etwa 1024 Bits. Das gilt unabhängig davon, ob $p,q$ “safe” sind.} \textcolor{yellow}{“$\approx$” ist hier qualitativ richtig; für die Bit-Analyse braucht man eine explizite Schranke auf $\delta$.}

Außerdem ist $ed = 1 + k\varphi(N)$ für ein $k \in \mathbb{N}$ mit $k < e$. Daraus folgt $d = (1 + k\varphi(N))/e$ für dieses $k$.

Die führenden Bits von $d$ können nun über die bereits erwähnte Approximation $\varphi(N) \approx N$ ermittelt werden, da damit $d \approx (1 + kN)/e$ ist. \textcolor{red}{So nicht ausreichend begründet: Aus der Approximation allein folgt noch nicht, welche Bits “genau” stimmen.} \textcolor{green}{Sauber: Schreibe $d=\frac{1+k(N-\delta)}{e}=\frac{kN+1}{e}-\frac{k\delta}{e}$. Weil $0<k<e$ und $\delta<2^{1024}$, gilt $0<\frac{k\delta}{e}<2^{1024}$. Das Abziehen eines Werts < $2^{1024}$ kann nur die untersten 1024 Bits beeinflussen. Damit stimmen die obersten $[\text{Bitlänge}(d)-1024]$ Bits von $d$ exakt mit denen von $\big\lfloor\frac{kN+1}{e}\big\rfloor$ überein. Da $N$ 2048 Bits und $e\approx 2^{16}$ hat, besitzt $d$ ca. 2032 Bits, also sind die obersten 2032−1024=1008 Bits unabhängig von $\delta$ exakt bestimmbar.}

Hierbei gibt es noch $\approx 2^{16}$ Möglichkeiten für $k$. \textcolor{green}{Diese kann man durchprobieren; für jedes $k$ lassen sich die obersten 1008 Bits von $d$ aus $\big\lfloor\frac{kN+1}{e}\big\rfloor$ direkt (ohne Kenntnis von $\varphi(N)$) berechnen.}

Eve kann sämtliche Möglichkeiten durchprobieren und dasjenige $k$ finden, das mit dem gegebenen Hash-Wert übereinstimmt. \textcolor{yellow}{Streng genommen vergleicht sie den SHA‑256 der ersten 1000 dieser 1008 hochrangigen Bits; Kollisionen sind theoretisch möglich, aber bei 256‑Bit‑Hash und nur 65537 Kandidaten vernachlässigbar.}

Auf diese Weise lernt sie etwa die ersten $1024$ Bits von $d$. \textcolor{red}{Falsch: Es sind (exakt) die ersten 1008 höchstwertigen Bits.} \textcolor{green}{Begründung: 2048 (Bits von $N$) − 16 (durch Division durch $e=65537$) − 1024 (Unsicherheit durch $\delta$) = 1008.} \textcolor{green}{Zusatz: Unabhängig vom Hash gilt wegen $\varphi(N)\equiv 0\pmod 4$ und $e\equiv 1\pmod 4$ auch $d\equiv 1\pmod 4$, d.h. die zwei niederwertigsten Bits von $d$ sind 01.}

Since nobody ever dies, and since everyone has at most two children, and since the gender ratio is 1:1, every generation will have exactly 1000 births (since each child has two parents). \textcolor{yellow}{“At most two” does not imply “exactly two”; you’re implicitly assuming everyone uses the full allowance and times both births around the generation time. State this.} \textcolor{red}{The justification “since each child has two parents” is not the reason; the correct argument is: with ≈500 women and 2 children per woman, you get ≈1000 births per 30-year cohort.} \textcolor{green}{More formally (discrete 30-year steps): if each woman has 2 children at ~30, then B_{g} = B_{g-1} (because B_{g} = 2 × number of 30-year-old women = 2 × 0.5 × B_{g-1}), so births per 30 years are constant. With 500 initial women, B = 1000 per 30 years.}

Therefore, initial growth is 1000 births every 30 years, which is a growth rate of 1000 / 30 ≈ 33.33 births per year. \textcolor{green}{This implies linear growth in total population because no one dies.}

After a billion years, the population will be roughly 1000 × 10^9 / 30 ≈ 3.33 × 10^{10} inhabitants. \textcolor{yellow}{Strictly P ≈ 1000 + (1000/30)·10^9; the +1000 is negligible at this scale.} \textcolor{green}{This estimate is “no-catastrophe” growth.}

This is clearly more than 10^{10}, so a Malthusian catastrophe will occur. The first such catastrophe will occur after 10^{10} / 33.33 ≈ 300 million years. \textcolor{yellow}{More precisely, (10^{10} − 10^{3}) / (1000/30) ≈ 3.0×10^8 years; your approximation is fine.}

It will reduce the population to about 10^9 people, the vast majority of whom will already have had two children; \textcolor{green}{Given constant births per 30-year bin over ~10^7 bins, almost everyone alive is >30, so “vast majority” is accurate under your age-at-birth assumption.}

assuming deaths in the catastrophic state are random, the first Malthusian catastrophe reduces future births to ≈ 3.33 per year, because it reduces the remaining legally breeding population by a factor of 10. \textcolor{red}{The mechanism is mis-stated. It’s not the stock of “legally breeding” people in general that sets births; it’s the size of the age-30 cohort (the pipeline). A one-time 90% cull cuts the cohorts that will reach age 30 by 10×, so after one generation the new constant becomes 100 births per 30 years, i.e., 100/30 ≈ 3.33 per year.} \textcolor{green}{Equivalently: if B_{g}=1000 just before, then B_{g+1}=0.1·B_{g}=100, and B_{g+2}=B_{g+1}, etc.}

A 700-million year climb follows which takes the final population to ≈ 10^9 + 3.33 · 7 · 10^8 ≈ 2.33 · 10^9. \textcolor{red}{Arithmetic: 3.33 × 7 × 10^8 ≈ 2.33 × 10^9, so P ≈ 10^9 + 2.33 × 10^9 = 3.33 × 10^9, not 2.33 × 10^9.} \textcolor{green}{So after 1 Gyr total time, P ≈ 3.33 × 10^9 (well below the 10^{10} threshold), hence only one catastrophe has occurred by then.}

In the long term, catastrophe states repeat at ever-increasing intervals, each reduces the breeding population by 90 percent, and the population finally hits a steady state at roughly one billion inhabitants. \textcolor{green}{Correct that intervals increase: after each catastrophe, the constant birth flow drops by 10× (1000 → 100 → 10 → …), so the time to add 9×10^9 people stretches by 10× each cycle (≈0.3 Gyr, 3 Gyr, 30 Gyr, …).} \textcolor{red}{There is no steady state at ≈10^9 under your “normal conditions resume” assumption. After each reset to 10^9, linear growth resumes (just more slowly) and the population again reaches 10^{10}, triggering another decimation. The system cycles between ≈10^9 and 10^{10} with ever-longer periods; it does not converge to 10^9.} \textcolor{green}{Summary: B_k = 1000·10^{-k} births per 30 years after the k-th catastrophe; intervals ≈ 2.7×10^8·10^{k} years between catastrophes.}

Submission: By Dirichlet's theorem on primes in arithmetic progressions, there are infinitely many primes both of the form $6k+1$ and $6k+5$. \textcolor{red}{This invokes a very advanced result for this course; an elementary approach is expected. It suffices to note there are infinitely many primes $\equiv 2 \pmod{3}$ (hence, except for 2, they are $\equiv 5 \pmod{6}$). This can be proved Euclid-style: if $p_1,\dots,p_n \equiv 2 \pmod{3}$, then $N=3p_1\cdots p_n-1\equiv 2 \pmod{3}$ has a new prime divisor $\equiv 2 \pmod{3}$. Then fix any prime $q\equiv 1 \pmod{6}$ (e.g., $q=7$).} 

Let $S_5$ and $S_7$ denote two such infinite sets. \textcolor{yellow}{Typo: should be $S_1$ (primes $\equiv 1 \pmod{6}$), not $S_7$.} \textcolor{green}{Also, you actually only need one infinite set (say $S_5$); take a fixed $q\equiv 1 \pmod{6}$.}

Then $p + q$ with $p \in S_5$ and $q \in S_7$ is always divisible by 6, \textcolor{green}{since $5+1\equiv 0 \pmod{6}$ and all primes $>3$ are $\equiv 1$ or $5 \pmod{6}$.} and there are infinitely many such pairs; this yields the desired result. \textcolor{yellow}{Clarify why infinitely many pairs give infinitely many distinct sums.} \textcolor{green}{For example, fix one $q$ and let $p$ vary over infinitely many elements of $S_5$; then $p+q$ are strictly increasing multiples of 6. Equivalently, each integer has only finitely many representations as a sum of two primes.}
